1. Project Management Practices for Centuries
- Project management is not a new concept. It has existed for thousands of years.
- Ancient people used their knowledge, technology, planning, and management methods to complete large projects.
- Examples:
  - Kala Wewa – built by King Dhathusena in the 5th century.
  - Yoda Ela – a major canal connected to the Kala Wewa reservoir project.
  - Great Pyramids of Egypt.
  - Great Wall of China.
- Ancient projects required:
  - Construction management
  - Logistics management
  - Planning and monitoring
  - Project execution and control
  - Project closure
Key point: Modern technology improves project management, but knowledge, planning, and good management are essential for project success.
2. Evolution of Project Management Techniques and Concepts
Four important techniques were introduced during the 20th century.
1917

Gantt Chart
- Invented by Henry Gantt.
- A horizontal bar chart showing project activities against time.
- Helps plan schedules and identify scheduling problems.



1957

Critical Path Method (CPM)
- Identifies the critical path, the longest-duration path through a project network.
- Determines the minimum time needed to complete the project.



1958

Program Evaluation and Review Technique (PERT)
- Developed by the American Navy.
- Helps estimate the time required to complete a project.



1962

Work Breakdown Structure (WBS)
- Divides the project scope into smaller, manageable components.
- Helps organize, control, and monitor project work.



Example – Burj Khalifa: Gantt charts were used for scheduling, CPM for identifying critical activities, and WBS for dividing project work into manageable components.
3. Impact of the Digital Age on Project Management
Before the digital age
- Communication was mainly verbal or handwritten.
- Documents were prepared and stored on paper.
- Gantt charts and network diagrams were drawn manually.
- Meetings were used to discuss project progress.
- Planning, scheduling, estimating, and monitoring were performed manually.
Example: Empire State Building construction (1931) used manual project management methods.
After the development of computers and the internet
- Email, chat, and video conferencing improved communication.
- Teams could collaborate remotely.
- Digital tools made project planning, tracking, and reporting easier.
- Project managers could manage projects more efficiently.
4. Impact of Personal Computers and Technology
Personal computers improved project management even before advanced software and high-speed internet became widely available.
- Scope control: Drawings, specifications, and requirement documents.
- Cost control: Spreadsheets, templates, and graphs.
- Communication: Email and internet.
- Efficiency: Faster information sharing and project management.
Example – Burj Khalifa: Building Information Modeling (BIM) helped create a virtual building model, manage scope, schedule and cost, coordinate stakeholders, and improve communication.
5. Software Applications in Modern Project Management
Project management software improves planning, scheduling, monitoring, and progress tracking.
Important software and dates from the lecture:
Software	Year
Microsoft Project	1990
Primavera Windows Version	1994
Main uses of project management software
- Activity planning
- Project scheduling
- Cost planning and estimation
- Resource planning
- Project monitoring
- Progress tracking
- Delay analysis
- Baselining
Baselining: Establishing an approved project plan that can be used to compare actual progress with planned progress.
PMBOK
Project Management Body of Knowledge (PMBOK) is a project management reference guide.
It recommends suitable software tools to support:
- Planning and scheduling
- Cost estimation
- Risk management
- Quality control
BIM (Building Information Modeling): Software-based 3D building modeling that helps identify design conflicts, reduce rework, and improve coordination and communication.
6. Evolution into an Analytical and Methodical Discipline
Modern project management has become more systematic and analytical.
Main developments:
- Better project management software and digital communication.
- Improved planning, monitoring, and control.
- Management of the complete project lifecycle.
- Development of different project management methodologies.
- Introduction of professional standards, knowledge domains, and ethical practices.
Project management methodologies
Waterfall (Predictive Approach)
- Plan-based approach.
- Work follows a predefined sequence and plan.


Agile (Adaptive Approach)
- Adapts to changing requirements.
- Commonly used in modern software development.


Modern example: In a skyscraper construction project, the project manager uses software, digital communication, lifecycle management, and appropriate project management methods to coordinate activities and monitor progress.

